.TH ESDM\-EGD\-NET\-RELAY 8 "2026-10-19" "ESDM @VERSION@" "ESDM Manual"
.SH NAME
esdm\-egd\-net\-relay \- serve the EGD socket of a host from an ESDM on the network
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B esdm\-egd\-net\-relay
.B \-\-connect
.I HOST[:PORT]
.B \-\-cert
.I FILE
.B \-\-key
.I FILE
.B \-\-ca
.I FILE
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.br
.B esdm\-egd\-net\-relay
.B \-\-connect
.I HOST[:PORT]
.B \-\-plain
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B esdm\-egd\-net\-relay
serves the EGD (Entropy Gathering Daemon) socket of a host that has no ESDM of
its own, and forwards every command to an
.BR esdm\-egd\-net\-server (8)
on another host. The consumers of the EGD protocol \- libgcrypt built with its
EGD module (rndegd), OpenSSL's
.BR RAND_egd (3),
the EGD provider of the ESDM, see
.BR esdm\-openssl\-provider (7)
\- know nothing but a UNIX domain socket, and find this one where they look for
the ESDM's:
.I @EGD_SOCKET_PATH@
by default (meson option
.BR esdm\-server\-egd\-socket\-path ).
They use the ESDM on the network unchanged.
.PP
Every local connection has an upstream connection of its own \- a client of
.BR libesdm_egd_client (3)
in its network mode \- which keeps the answers in the order the consumer
expects. The library's reconnect makes a restart of the server between two
commands invisible to the consumer. The relay answers the get PID command
(0x04) with its own PID and the privileged commands (0xe6 to 0xea) with
\-EPERM itself; everything else is answered by the server, which treats every
client as unprivileged: an entropy claim attached to inserted data is dropped.
.PP
A consumer confined by seccomp keeps its syscall discipline with the relay \-
it speaks to a UNIX domain socket, see
.BR libesdm_egd_client (3)
\- which the TLS mode of the library does not keep.
.PP
Upstream, the same two modes as the server:
.TP
.B TLS
TLS 1.3 with mutual authentication: the relay presents its certificate, and
verifies that of the server against the trust anchors of
.BR \-\-ca ,
that it is issued for the name of the server \- the host of
.B \-\-connect
or
.BR \-\-server_name ,
a DNS name or an IP address \- and, if it has an extended key usage, for TLS
server authentication. With
.BR \-\-crl ,
the server's chain is checked exactly as the server checks the clients', see
.B REVOCATION
in
.BR esdm\-egd\-net\-server (8).
The algorithms \- the hybrid key exchange of ML\-KEM, and no post\-quantum
cryptography on its own \- are those of the server, see
.B TLS
in
.BR esdm\-egd\-net\-server (8).
.TP
.B plain TCP
Neither encrypted nor authenticated: only for a network whose transport is
protected underneath, by IPsec or WireGuard, say. Only with
.BR \-\-plain ,
which refuses any TLS option next to it; with neither TLS files nor
.B \-\-plain
the relay refuses to start. A warning is logged at the start.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-C ", " \-\-connect " \fIHOST[:PORT]\fR"
The server: a name or an address, an IPv6 address in brackets, as in
.BR [2001:db8::1]:@EGD_NET_PORT@ .
The port defaults to @EGD_NET_PORT@. The name is resolved at every connection,
and its addresses are tried in turn. Required.
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-socket " \fIPATH\fR"
The local socket, created with mode 0666 \- the consumers are applications of
every user. A socket left behind by an earlier relay is replaced, one served
by a running process is not. The default is
.IR @EGD_SOCKET_PATH@ ,
unless a socket is handed over by socket activation, which is served then.
.TP
.B \-\-plain
Plain TCP upstream, see above.
.TP
.BI \-\-cert " FILE"
Certificate of the relay, PEM, optionally followed by the intermediate
certificates of its chain. It has to chain to the trust anchors of
.BR \-\-ca .
.TP
.BI \-\-key " FILE"
Its private key, PEM, unencrypted, PKCS#8.
.TP
.BI \-\-ca " FILE"
Trust anchors the server's certificate has to chain to, PEM. A chain ends in a
self\-signed one, see
.B \-\-ca
in
.BR esdm\-egd\-net\-server (8).
.TP
.BI \-\-server_name " NAME"
Name the server's certificate has to be issued for, when it is not the host of
.BR \-\-connect .
.TP
.BI \-\-crl " FILE"
CRLs, PEM; a file may hold several. May be given up to 16 times. Complete CRLs
only, no delta CRLs nor ones with an issuing distribution point \- see
.B REVOCATION
in
.BR esdm\-egd\-net\-server (8).
.TP
.B \-\-crl_check_all
Require CRLs of the CA certificates of the server's chain as well.
.TP
.B \-\-crl_allow_expired
Use a CRL past its nextUpdate with a warning.
.TP
.BI \-\-reload_interval " SEC"
Check the TLS files for a change this often \- to report a rotation that does
not load right away and the CRLs close to or past their nextUpdate, and to
check the established upstream connections against the CRLs, see
.BR REVOCATION .
New upstream connections check the files themselves, see
.BR ROTATION .
0 checks them on
.B SIGHUP
only. The default is 60.
.TP
.BI \-\-timeout " SEC"
Bound of a step upstream \- making a connection with its handshake, or a
transfer. A blocking read waits that long for an answer of the server, which
itself waits for the ESDM to become operational. The default is 30.
.TP
.BI \-\-max_connections " N"
Local connections at a time, in all. The default is 64. Each takes a connection
to the server of its own, all of them with the certificate of the relay \- in
plain mode, from its address: the server serves no more of them than its
.B \-\-max_per_client
allows, 64 by default, and refuses the connections beyond, which fails the
requests of the consumers whose turn it is. Keep this at or below that bound
of the server.
.IP
With all of them taken, a new connection closes one of the user holding the
most \- the one its consumer used least recently \- as the EGD socket of
.BR esdm\-server (8)
does: a few users filling the slots cannot lock anybody else out, and a new
connection of a user holding the most costs that user one of its own. Root's
connections are not closed; a new connection that would close no other is
refused.
.TP
.BI \-\-max_per_uid " N"
Local connections per user \- the subordinate UIDs of a user count as the user,
as for the EGD socket of
.BR esdm\-server (8);
root is exempt. The default is 16.
.TP
.BI \-\-idle_timeout " SEC"
A local connection whose consumer sent nothing for this long is closed; 0 for
never, the default: a consumer confined by seccomp after connecting cannot
connect again.
.TP
.BI \-\-io_timeout " SEC"
Bound of the rest of a command once it started, in all from its first byte,
and of a consumer taking its answer. The default is 30.
.TP
.BR \-u ", " \-\-username " \fIUSER\fR"
User to switch to after the socket is bound, when started as root. The default
is
.BR nobody .
The TLS files are read as that user by every new upstream connection.
.TP
.BR \-g ", " \-\-groupname " \fIGROUP\fR"
Supplementary group to switch to. The default is none.
.TP
.BR \-S ", " \-\-syslog
Log to syslog.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
More verbose logging, may be repeated. Warnings and errors are logged without.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print the usage.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version.
.PP
A file named without a slash is a credential of the service, taken from
.BR $CREDENTIALS_DIRECTORY ;
without
.BR \-\-cert ,
.B \-\-key
and
.BR \-\-ca ,
in TLS mode, the credentials
.BR cert ,
.B key
and
.B ca
are used.
.SH ROTATION
Client certificates expire, too. Each new upstream connection \- that of a new
local consumer, and the reconnect of an existing one \- checks the TLS files
for a change, by the path, device, inode, size and modification time of each,
and loads them anew when they changed. Only files that load completely \- the
key belongs to the certificate, the certificate chains to the trust anchors,
the CRLs parse \- replace the ones in use; otherwise the error is logged and
the connections go on with what was loaded before. Established connections
keep the certificate they were made with.
.PP
The credentials of
.B LoadCredential=
are copies made when the service starts; a rotation without a restart needs
the paths of the files, readable by the user of the service. The CRLs are
downloaded as described in
.B CRL UPDATES
of
.BR esdm\-egd\-net\-server (8).
.SH REVOCATION
Consumers hold their connection for their whole life, and with it their
upstream connection, so revocation has to bite on established connections
too: after every reload of the TLS files, the relay checks the chains of its
upstream connections against the new CRLs and closes those to a revoked
server \- one whose handshake ran during the reload is checked once it
completed. Every 30 seconds, it checks them again and closes those whose server
certificate expired. Both are logged with the subject of the server. A
connection in the middle of a command is checked before the next one.
.PP
The consumer keeps its local connection: its next command makes a new
upstream connection, whose handshake the server has to pass with the files as
they are then \- a revoked server fails it, and the command with it.
.SH SIGNALS
.TP
.B SIGHUP
Check the TLS files at once, report what is wrong with them, and check the
upstream connections against the CRLs, see
.BR REVOCATION .
.TP
.BR SIGTERM ", " SIGINT
Close the connections and exit with status 0.
.SH SYSTEMD
.B esdm\-egd\-net\-relay.socket
creates the socket \- and conflicts with
.BR esdm\-server\-egd.socket ,
the EGD socket of a local ESDM, which it replaces \- and starts
.BR esdm\-egd\-net\-relay.service ,
which runs as a user of its own, loads the TLS files as credentials from
.I /etc/esdm/egd\-net/client.crt ,
.I client.key
and
.IR ca.crt ,
and takes its options, the server among them, from
.B ESDM_EGD_NET_RELAY_ARGS
of
.IR /etc/esdm/egd\-net\-relay.env :
.PP
.nf
ESDM_EGD_NET_RELAY_ARGS="\-\-connect egd.example"
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
esdm\-egd\-net\-relay \-\-connect egd.example:@EGD_NET_PORT@ \\
	\-\-cert /etc/esdm/egd\-net/client.crt \\
	\-\-key /etc/esdm/egd\-net/client.key \\
	\-\-ca /etc/esdm/egd\-net/ca.crt

esdm\-egd\-net\-relay \-\-plain \-\-connect 10.8.0.1
.fi
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 after
.BR SIGTERM ,
1 when the options, the TLS files or the socket do not allow it to start.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR esdm\-egd\-net\-server (8),
.BR libesdm_egd_client (3),
.BR esdm\-server (8),
.BR esdm\-openssl\-provider (7)
.SH AUTHOR
Markus Theil <theil.markus@gmail.com>
